content.thinking:
I see the issue now. Let me trace the problem.

Looking at `stringifyName`:

```typescript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The problem is that `next.value[0]` for astral characters (code points > 0xFFFF) returns a lone surrogate half, not the actual character. Since JavaScript strings are UTF-16, `str[0]` for a string starting with `𐌱` (U+10331) would return the high surrogate `\uD800`, which doesn't match `\p{ID_Continue}`.

So the fix is to properly extract the first code point from the string when testing against `ID_CONTINUE`. We need to use something like `[...str][0]` or `String.fromCodePoint(str.codePointAt(0))` to get the full code point.

Let me verify this is the issue:

arguments.content:
// Verify the root cause
const ID_CONTINUE = /^[$\u200c\u200d\p{ID_Continue}]$/u;

