Wait, this is interesting. The manual test shows that with my fix, the param name IS being quoted when followed by astral ID_Continue text:

```
Manual stringify: /:"name"𐌱rest
```

This means the param name is quoted, and when reparsed, the param name is preserved as "name" (not "name𐌱").

But wait, the output shows`/:name"𐌱rest` not`/:name"𐌱"rest`. Let me look at the stringifyName function again:

```javascript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(String.fromCodePoint(next.value.codePointAt(0)))) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

When the param name is valid ID and the next text starts with ID_Continue, it returns `quoteName(name)`. `quoteName` wraps the name in double quotes:

```javascript
function quoteName(name: string): string {
  return `"${name.replace(/["\\]/g, "\\$&")}"`;
}
```

So for param name "name", `quoteName("name")` returns `"name"`.

And in `stringifyTokens`:
```javascript
if (token.type === "param") {
  value += ":" + stringifyName(token.name, tokens[index]);
  continue;
}
```

So the output would be `:` + `"name"` = `:"name"`.

The full string would be `/` + `:"name"` + `𐌱rest` = `/:name"𐌱rest`.

This looks correct! The param name is quoted as `"name"`, and the text starts with `𐌱`.


When reparsing, the parser encounters `:`, sees the opening quote, reads until the closing quote to extract `name`, then encounters `𐌱rest` as text—so the tokens match the original. The fix works as intended. I should verify this doesn't break existing tests.

